      "math_academy_id": "q-253558",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Consider the set $B=(\\pi ,4).$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $inf(B).$\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of $B.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $B$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The infimum of $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le \\pi $ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,\\pi ]$ contains only lower bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>\\pi $ is **not** a lower bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>\\pi .$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,\\pi ]$ is the set of lower bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\ninf(B)=max(-\\infty ,\\pi ]=\\pi .\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\pi \\notin B.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $B$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\le \\pi \\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $inf(B)=\\pi \\notin B.$",
